#### 3.3.3.1 \(<\)\(K\)\(>\) / \(<\)\(\pi\)\(>\) ratio

The \(<\)\(K\)\(>\) / \(<\)\(\pi\)\(>\) ratio is an interesting measurement as it links the production of kaons, which carry the majority of the produced strangeness in the collision, with the production of pions, which are the most abundant of the produced particles and can be said to measure the entropy of the collision.2 An increase in the \(<\)\(K\)\(>\) / \(<\)\(\pi\)\(>\) ratio in A+A collisions compared to p+p or p+A collisions could then indicate an increase in the production of strangeness, where the definitions of \(<\)\(K\)\(>\) and \(<\)\(\pi\)\(>\) are given in equations (3.19) and (3.20).

