### 8.1 \(\Lambda\)/\(\Lambda\) Ratio

By investigating the anti-baryon to baryon ratio, much can be learned about the initial conditions of the collision. If the amount of baryon stopping is large, then there is an abundance of quarks in the fireball from the participating nucleons. It is then easier to produce a \(\Lambda\) (\(uds\)) with a \(K^{+}\) (\(u\overline{s}\)) through the reaction N+N-\(>\)N+\(\Lambda\) +\(K^{+}\) (known as associated production), than a \(\Lambda\) and an \(\overline{\Lambda}\) (\(\overline{u}\overline{d}\overline{s}\)) through the reaction N+N-\(>\)N+N+\(\Lambda\) +\(\overline{\Lambda}\) (known as pair production). The \(\overline{\Lambda}\) /\(\Lambda\) ratio is governed by the relative rates of associated production and pair production, as given in equation 8.1, where \(Y_{\Lambda}\) represents the \(\Lambda\) yield, \(Y_{\overline{\Lambda}}\) the \(\overline{\Lambda}\) yield, and \(Y_{pp}\) and \(Y_{ap}\) the yields from pair production and associated production respectively.

\[\frac{Y_{\overline{\Lambda}}}{Y_{\Lambda}}=\frac{Y_{pp}}{Y_{pp}+Y_{ap}} \tag{8.1}\]

In a baryon-rich scenario, the baryo-chemical potential (\(\mu_{B}\)) is large, and the production of anti-baryons is suppressed, therefore associated production dominates the \(\Lambda\) yield. As \(\mu_{B}\) reduces, the relative importance of associated production to pair production decreases and the \(\overline{\Lambda}\) /\(\Lambda\) ratio tends to 1. Therefore, a measure of the \(\overline{\Lambda}\) /\(\Lambda\) ratio is sensitive to the net baryon density at mid-rapidity in the collision. Also, due to the predicted strangeness enhancement in a QGP, the values of the strange \(\overline{\rm B}\)/B ratios may increase, for a constant temperature and a constant \(\mu_{B}\)[102].

When measuring \(\overline{\Lambda}\) and \(\Lambda\) particles in the detector, the values reported also include the yield of \(\Sigma^{0}\) (\(uds\)) and \(\overline{\Sigma^{0}}\) (\(\overline{u}\overline{d}\overline{s}\)) respectively. These decay electromagnetically through the channels \(\Sigma^{0}\rightarrow\Lambda\)\(\gamma\) and \(\overline{\Sigma^{0}}\rightarrow\overline{\Lambda}\)\(\gamma\), with a mean lifetime (\(c\tau\)) of 2.22 x 10\({}^{-11}\)m and a branching fraction of 100\(\%\), which means that they cannot be distinguished experimentally from primary \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) hyperons. However, this is the case for all experiments, so comparisons of yields and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) /\(\Lambda\) ratios with other experimental values are valid, but care has to be taken when comparing to theoretical models and predictions, where \(\Sigma^{0}\) and \(\Lambda\) may be treated separately.

As the acceptance of the TPC is symmetric with respect to both charge signs, the efficiency of finding an \(\overline{\Lambda}\) is approximately the same as that for a \(\Lambda\). Therefore, raw \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) yields can be used to determine the \(\overline{\Lambda}\) /\(\Lambda\) ratio without applying any of the corrections discussed in the previous chapter. The only difference is the absorption of both \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) hyperons which occurs with the material in the detector. This effecthas been studied using the Monte-Carlo (MC) generated \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) particles used in embedding. The absorption effect is greatest for the \(\overline{\Lambda}\), as expected, and is approximately constant as a function of transverse momentum and rapidity in the measured range. The average value of the correction to the ratio itself is 0.990 \(\pm\) 0.008 and has been applied to all results quoted in this chapter.

#### Integrated \(\overline{\Lambda}\) /\(\Lambda\) Ratio

Figure 8.2 shows a plot of the \(\overline{\Lambda}\) mass (red) superimposed onto the \(\Lambda\) mass (black), and it represents the full statistics available in the rapidity range \(|y|\)\(<\) 0.5, integrated over all transverse momenta. From this, the value of the \(\overline{\Lambda}\) /\(\Lambda\) ratio after the absorption correction was applied, was determined to be : 0.736 \(\pm\) 0.008 (statistical). This suggests, utilising equation (8.1), that at mid-rapidity, approximately 74\(\%\) of \(\Lambda\) come from pair production, and approximately 26\(\%\) come from associated production.

The \(\overline{\Lambda}\) /\(\Lambda\) ratio behaviour was also investigated as a function of different physical variables. In order to accomplish this, the data were divided into different bins of

