#### Rapidity Distributions

The total integrated yields for the \(K^{0}_{ S}\), \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\), presented in Tables 8.3 - 8.5 were measured in the rapidity interval -0.5 \(<\) y \(<\) 0.5. As the acceptance of all three particle species extends to approximately \(\mid\) y \(\mid\)\(<\)1.2 (shown in Figure 7.11), the \(dN/d\)y was calculated as a function of y in the range \(\mid\) y \(\mid\)\(<\)1. These distributions are shown in Figure 8.12 and Figure 8.13.

It is evident from this that there is no significant deviation from a flat distribution for the \(K^{0}_{ S}\). The errors are necessarily larger than the value given in Table 8.3 as the available statistics per bin were a factor of four smaller than used previously. On the other hand, both the \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) distributions show a rise at larger rapidities. It has been seen in previous experiments that as expected, all \(\overline{\Lambda}\) hyperons are produced at mid-rapidity [106]. This dip therefore indicates that some unresolved problems could exist in determining the \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) spectra.

#### Reproducibility of the Data by the Simulation

In order to be sure that the correction factors being applied are correct, the simulation must be able to reproduce the data well. In order to assess this, several checks were made. The first method was to compare the distributions of the cut variables in the MC to those in the raw data, the next method compared how well the MC reproduced the data in different physical areas in the detector, and the final method was to perform a lifetime correction on the data, as the result in this case is known.

##### Comparison of Cut Variables

The cut variables should have the same distribution, in both the reconstructed MC data, as well as in the reconstructed raw data, so that when cuts are applied, they have the same effect in the MC as in the data. These cut-variable distributions are shown in Figure 8.14 for the \(K^{0}_{ S}\), Figure 8.15 for the \(\Lambda\) and Figure 8.16 for the \(\overline{\Lambda}\). In all three figures, the blue distributions represent the raw reconstructed data, and the red distributions represent the reconstructed embedded data, and all distributions are normalised to unity.

Of all the cut distributions that were investigated, the only one which showed significant differences were the DCAs of the parent V0 to the primary vertex, though the difference was much greater for the \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) compared to the \(K^{0}_{ S}\). Although the hits distributions are also slightly different, the mean values are only different by 1 hit, with the real data tracks having more hits that the reconstructed embedded tracks. As the cut is placed on the tail of the hit distributions, then the difference that this offset in the distributions makes is small.

Unlike the \(K^{0}_{ S}\), both the \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) particles that are measured are not necessarily primary particles. The \(\Lambda\) may also originate from decays of the \(\Xi^{-}\), \(\Xi^{0}\) and \(\Omega^{-}\), whilst the \(\overline{\Lambda}\) may come from \(\overline{\Xi^{+}}\), \(\overline{\Xi^{0}}\) and \(\overline{\Omega^{+}}\) decays, and these secondary particles are known as feed-down particles. In the cases of the charged parent particles, the primary particles will bend in the magnetic field and will decay away from the primary vertex with a mean lifetime of 4.91 cm in the cases of the \(\Xi^{-}\) and \(\overline{\Xi^{+}}\), and 2.46 cm for the \(\Omega^{-}\) and \(\overline{\Omega^{+}}\). This, combined with a momentum kick in the decay which comes from the mass differences between the parent and daughter particles, means that the secondary \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) will have a different DCA distribution at the primaryFigure 8.14: Distributions of the various cut variables for the \(K^{0}_{ S}\). The blue distributions represent the reconstructed raw data, the red distributions are for the reconstructed MC data.

Figure 8.15: Distributions of the various cut variables for the \(\Lambda\). The blue distributions represent the reconstructed raw data, the red distributions are for the reconstructed MC data.

Figure 8.16: Distributions of the various cut variables for the \(\overline{\Lambda}\). The blue distributions represent the reconstructed raw data, the red distributions are for the reconstructed MC data.

vertex. The momentum kick in the decay of the neutral \(\Xi^{0}\) and \(\Xi^{0}\), which do not bend in the magnetic field, also means that the secondary \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) from these decays do not point back to the primary vertex. The effect of secondary contamination in the raw \(\Lambda\) (\(\overline{\Lambda}\)) yield is highlighted in Figures 8.15 and 8.16, because while the raw data distributions include secondary \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\), the embedding only contains primary \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\).

Figure 8.17 shows how the DCA distribution of the \(\Lambda\) to the primary vertex compares between what is seen in the raw data, and those from \(\Lambda\), \(\Xi^{-}\), \(\Xi^{0}\) and \(\Omega^{-}\) embedding.

It can be seen that the magnitude of the broadening of the measured distribution in the raw data is dependent upon the number of reconstructed primary and secondary \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\). It is therefore not a systematic error and is simply a feed-down effect. This is discussed in more detail in section 8.2.6.

